\section{The draws}{

\code{posterior} holds one named block per group of parameters, and each
block is a list whose \code{coeffs} carries the draws:

\describe{
  \item{\code{a}}{The coefficients. \code{coeffs} has \eqn{M} columns, or
  \eqn{TM} with \code{tvp = TRUE}, the whole path of a draw stacked by period.
  \code{lambda} holds the inclusion indicators where variable selection is on
  and \code{sigma} the variances of the random walk where the coefficients
  drift.}
  \item{\code{u_sigma_inv}}{The error precision matrix, \eqn{K^2} columns per
  period, vectorised. \code{sigma} holds the variance of the log-volatility
  innovations under stochastic volatility, which is what a forecast steps the
  volatility forward by.}
  \item{\code{u_omega_inv}}{The diagonal of that precision, \eqn{K} columns
  per period, for every error but \code{"wishart"}.}
  \item{\code{psi}}{The triangular matrix of the covariance block, \eqn{K^2}
  columns -- the whole matrix, not only its free elements -- with
  \code{lambda} and \code{sigma} as above.}
  \item{\code{u_scale}}{The scale of the asymmetric Laplace, \eqn{K} columns,
  for a quantile VAR.}
  \item{\code{beta}}{The cointegration vectors of a VEC model,
  \eqn{k_\beta r} columns, and \code{rho} where a time-varying cointegration
  space put a prior on its state autoregression.}
  \item{\code{q}}{The rotations of a sign-restricted identification, added by
  \code{\link{add_sign_restrictions}}.}
  \item{\code{loglik}}{The pointwise log-likelihood of the estimation sample,
  one column per period, added by \code{\link{add_posterior_loglik}}. A matrix
  of draws rather than a block with \code{coeffs}, since there is only one
  thing to hold.}
  \item{\code{forecast}}{Everything the forecast periods produce:
  \code{forecasts}, the simulated paths from
  \code{\link{add_posterior_forecasts}}, and \code{errors}, what
  \code{\link{add_forecast_errors}} took against \code{data$test$y}. Both have
  \eqn{Kh} columns, stacked by period. The members are named after what they
  hold rather than one of them being \code{draws}, because all of them are
  draws.}
}
}

\section{Two conventions worth knowing}{

\strong{Draws are rows.} Every matrix of draws is a
\code{\link[coda]{mcmc}} object with one row per draw and one column per
parameter, carrying the \code{start}, \code{end} and \code{thin} of the chain.
A posterior mean is \code{colMeans()} and a credible interval a column
quantile; transposing to "fix" the shape gives a summary of the wrong thing.
\code{\link{bvar}} and \code{\link{bvec}}, which collect the draws of a
sampler written by hand, are the exception and take the transpose.

\strong{A period is a block of columns.} Where a quantity moves with time, the
path of one draw is stacked by period along that draw's row, so period
\eqn{t} of a \eqn{K^2} wide block is columns \eqn{(t-1)K^2 + 1} to
\eqn{tK^2}. The same holds of a forecast: the \eqn{K} variables of the first
horizon come first, then those of the second.
}

\section{Lists of models}{

Passing a vector to an argument such as \code{p}, \code{r} or \code{quantile}
gives one model per specification in a list of class \code{'modellist'}, and
\code{\link{use_expanding_window}} a list of class \code{'expandingwindow'},
one model per window. Every step of the workflow has a method for both and
applies to each model in turn, so the structure above is what is inside either
of them. \code{\link{open_models}} is the same idea for a folder too large to
hold: a handle to the files rather than the models.
}

\section{The same layout on disk}{

\code{\link{write_to_hdf5}} writes the object to an HDF5 file with the paths
the elements above are named by -- \code{/data/train/y}, \code{/data/test/y},
\code{/posterior/a/coeffs}, \code{/posterior/forecast/forecasts} -- and
\code{\link{read_model_from_hdf5}} reads it back. It is the format the BayesTS
command line runs on, which is what makes a model estimated here and a model
estimated there the same object.

In the file every dataset is one row per quantity and one column per draw.
R's readers reverse the dimension order, so a session sees the transpose of
that, which is the draws-in-rows layout above.
}

